# .NET C# Best Practices (Backend)

- Prefer clear, small methods and single-responsibility classes.
- Prefer concise methods always target methods no longer than 30 lines of code. If the method is long, extract private methods so that upper level has higher-level concepts while downstream methods go to low-level specifics.
- Use dependency injection; avoid `new` for infrastructure dependencies inside services/controllers.
- Use async end-to-end for I/O (`Task`/`Task<T>`), pass `CancellationToken`, and avoid `.Result`/`.Wait()`.
- Validate inputs at boundaries; return explicit, meaningful errors.
- Never swallow exceptions; log with context and either handle or rethrow.
- Use `var` only when the type is obvious from the right-hand side.
- Favor immutable DTOs/records for request/response contracts.
- Use nullable reference types correctly; avoid `!` unless truly safe and documented.
- Keep business logic out of controllers; place it in application/domain services.
- Write tests for critical behavior and edge cases before marking work complete.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
public IActionResult Get(int id) {
    var order = _repo.GetAsync(id).Result;
    return Ok(order);
}

// ✅ GOOD
public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id, CancellationToken ct) {
    var order = await _repo.GetAsync(id, ct);
    return order is null ? NotFound() : Ok(order);
}
```

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
catch (Exception) { }

// ✅ GOOD
catch (Exception ex) {
    _logger.LogError(ex, "Failed to process order {OrderId}", orderId);
    throw;
}
```

